- How would you handle skewed category distributions and ensure stability during microbursts?
- What are the trade-offs of using a single global heap vs per-category heaps for top-K queries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming state with sliding windows, using hash maps, per-id deques (linked lists), and a heap for ranking. Emphasizes correctness under out-of-order arrivals and lazy expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window maintenance per key using a deque
- Lazy expiration and lazy heap invalidation
- Top-K extraction via a max-heap with versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
class RealTimeTopKWindow {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.byId = new Map(); // id -> { deque: [], sum, version }
    this.globalMax = new MinHeap((a,b)=>b.sum-a.sum); // max-heap emulation
  }
  addEvent(id, value, ts) {
    // Ensure id exists
    const rec = this.byId.get(id) || { deque: [], sum: 0, version: 0 };
    // expire old
    while (rec.deque.length && rec.deque[0].ts <= ts - this.W) {
      const old = rec.deque.shift();
      rec.sum -= old.value;
    }
    rec.deque.push({ ts, value });
    rec.sum += value;
    rec.version++;
    this.byId.set(id, rec);
    this.globalMax.push({ sum: rec.sum, id, ver: rec.version });
  }
  getTopK(K) {
    const res = [];
    const seen = new Set();
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.globalMax.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const cur = this.byId.get(top.id);
      if (!cur || cur.version !== top.ver || top.sum !== cur.sum) continue; // stale
      if (!seen.has(top.id)) {
        res.push(top.id);
        seen.add(top.id);
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if W varies by id? 
- How to scale to millions of ids with bounded memory?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to implement a Trie with efficient prefix queries, which is fundamental for autocomplete features. The per-node counter design enables O(k) prefix counting where k is the prefix length, and O(m) insertion where m is the word length.

## Key Concepts

- Trie structure with per-node subtree counts
- Incrementing counts during insertion for O(1) prefix queries
- Prefix traversal and counting
- Edge case handling for empty strings, null inputs, and non-existent prefixes

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeCount = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (!word || typeof word !== 'string') {
      return;
    }

    let node = this.root;
    node.subtreeCount++;

    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeCount++;
    }

    node.isWord = true;
  }

  getPrefixCount(prefix) {
    if (!prefix || typeof prefix !== 'string') {
      return 0;
    }

    let node = this.root;

    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return 0;
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    return node.subtreeCount;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('cat');
trie.insert('cater');
trie.insert('category');
trie.insert('dog');

console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('cat'));  // 3 (cat, cater, category)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('ca'));   // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('c'));    // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('dog'));  // 1
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('z'));    // 0 (non-existent prefix)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(''));     // 0 (empty string)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(null));   // 0 (null input)
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity**: O(m) for insert, O(k) for getPrefixCount where m is word length and k is prefix length
- **Space Complexity**: O(ALPHABET_SIZE × m × n) where m is average word length and n is number of words, but optimized with Map-based children

