## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Is Asked
This tests building a Trie and adding an autocomplete-style suggest method, a common real-world need.

## Key Concepts
- Trie nodes with 26 children; end-of-word markers; storing full word for suggestions
- Lexicographic DFS for deterministic suggestions
- Complexity and memory trade-offs; edge cases like no matches

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor(){ this.children = new Map(); this.isWord=false; this.word=null }
}
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word){ let n=this.root; for (let ch of word){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) n.children.set(ch,new TrieNode()); n=n.children.get(ch); } n.isWord=true; n.word=word; }
  search(word){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return false; n=n.children.get(ch); } return n.isWord; }
  startsWith(prefix){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return false; n=n.children.get(ch); } return true; }
  suggest(prefix, limit){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return []; n=n.children.get(ch); }
    const res=[];
    const dfs=(cur, path)=>{ if(res.length>=limit) return; if(cur.isWord) res.push(prefix+path); const keys=Array.from(cur.children.keys()).sort(); for(let c of keys){ dfs(cur.children.get(c), path+c); } };
    dfs(n, '');
    return res;
  }
}
```

